### What happens when a broker's disk fills up?

Kafka stores each partition replica as log segment files plus index files in a configured log directory on local disk. When that directory's filesystem runs out of space, any attempt to append a record or extend an index fails with an IO exception. Kafka marks that log directory offline, takes the partitions hosted there out of service, and may shut the broker down.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.*;
import org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Properties;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutionException;

public class DiskFullProducerDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(ProducerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "kafka-1:9092");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.KEY_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringSerializer.class.getName());
        props.put(ProducerConfig.VALUE_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringSerializer.class.getName());
        props.put(ProducerConfig.ACKS_CONFIG, "all");

        try (KafkaProducer<String, String> producer = new KafkaProducer<>(props)) {
            try {
                producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("transfers", "SAV2001",
                    "DEBIT " + new BigDecimal("50000"))).get();
                System.out.println("Transfer recorded successfully");
            } catch (ExecutionException e) {
                // When broker disk is full, producer gets this error
                System.out.println("ERROR: " + e.getCause().getClass().getSimpleName());
                System.out.println("Broker disk may be full -- transfer not recorded");
                // NotEnoughReplicasException or TimeoutException
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output (when broker disk is full):
ERROR: NotEnoughReplicasException
Broker disk may be full -- transfer not recorded
```

### How does disk full affect replication and durability?

Replication is affected because a follower on the full broker cannot write the data it fetches, so it stops making progress and is removed from the in-sync replica set. This reduces the redundancy of every partition that had a replica there, so a second failure becomes more dangerous.

| Scenario | Impact |
|---|---|
| Leader on full broker | Writes fail, producers get errors |
| Follower on full broker | Falls out of ISR, reduces durability |
| All replicas full | Partition completely unavailable |
| Other brokers healthy | Kafka elects new leader from ISR |

```bash
# Check which partitions are under-replicated after disk full
kafka-topics.sh --describe --under-replicated-partitions \
  --bootstrap-server kafka-2:9092
```

```text
Topic: transfers  Partition: 3  Leader: 2  Replicas: 1,2,3  Isr: 2,3
  (Broker 1 dropped from ISR -- disk full)
Topic: transfers  Partition: 7  Leader: 2  Replicas: 1,2,3  Isr: 2,3
```

### How do you prevent disk full with retention settings?

You set `retention.ms` or `retention.bytes` per topic so old data is deleted on a schedule. The log cleaner runs periodically based on `log.retention.check.interval.ms`.

```bash
# Set retention to 7 days and 100 GB max for the transfers topic
kafka-topics.sh --alter --topic transfers \
  --config retention.ms=604800000 \
  --config retention.bytes=107374182400 \
  --bootstrap-server kafka-1:9092

# Verify retention settings
kafka-configs.sh --describe --topic transfers \
  --bootstrap-server kafka-1:9092
```

```text
Dynamic configs for topic transfers:
  retention.ms=604800000
  retention.bytes=107374182400
```

### How do you monitor disk usage and set alerts?

Monitor disk usage per log directory and alert well before full, commonly at seventy or eighty percent, so operators can act before the broker goes offline.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import java.io.File;

public class DiskMonitor {
    public static void checkDiskUsage(String logDir) {
        File dir = new File(logDir);
        long total = dir.getTotalSpace();
        long free = dir.getFreeSpace();
        long used = total - free;
        double usagePercent = (double) used / total * 100;

        System.out.printf("Kafka log dir: %s%n", logDir);
        System.out.printf("  Total: %.1f GB%n", total / 1e9);
        System.out.printf("  Used:  %.1f GB (%.1f%%)%n", used / 1e9, usagePercent);
        System.out.printf("  Free:  %.1f GB%n", free / 1e9);

        if (usagePercent > 80) {
            System.out.println("  ALERT: Disk usage above 80%% -- take action!");
        } else if (usagePercent > 70) {
            System.out.println("  WARNING: Disk usage above 70%% -- monitor closely");
        } else {
            System.out.println("  OK: Disk usage within safe limits");
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        checkDiskUsage("/var/kafka/data");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Kafka log dir: /var/kafka/data
  Total: 500.0 GB
  Used:  410.0 GB (82.0%)
  Free:  90.0 GB
  ALERT: Disk usage above 80% -- take action!
```

### How do you use quotas to prevent runaway producers?

Producer quotas stop a runaway producer from filling disks faster than cleanup frees space. You set byte-rate limits per client id or per user.

```bash
# Set a 10 MB/s produce quota for the batch-import client
kafka-configs.sh --alter --add-config 'producer_byte_rate=10485760' \
  --entity-type clients --entity-name batch-import \
  --bootstrap-server kafka-1:9092
```

### How do you recover from a disk full situation?

Recovery means freeing space or adding it. Reduce retention temporarily so the cleaner deletes more, add disk capacity, or add brokers and reassign partitions. Never hand-delete segment files, which is risky; let Kafka manage cleanup through retention settings.

```bash
# Emergency: reduce retention temporarily to free space
kafka-topics.sh --alter --topic transfers \
  --config retention.ms=86400000 \
  --bootstrap-server kafka-1:9092
# After space is freed, restore normal retention
kafka-topics.sh --alter --topic transfers \
  --config retention.ms=604800000 \
  --bootstrap-server kafka-1:9092

# Check that the affected replicas have rejoined the ISR
kafka-topics.sh --describe --topic transfers --bootstrap-server kafka-1:9092
```

### What are common mistakes in disk management?

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class DiskManagementMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // WRONG: manually deleting segment files
        System.out.println("WRONG: rm /var/kafka/data/transfers-3/*.log");
        System.out.println("  Risk: corrupts partition, broker may crash");

        // CORRECT: reduce retention and let Kafka clean up
        System.out.println("CORRECT: kafka-topics --alter --config retention.ms=86400000");
        System.out.println("  Safe: Kafka's log cleaner handles deletion properly");

        // WRONG: Kafka data on same volume as OS
        System.out.println("WRONG: /var/kafka/data on root filesystem");
        System.out.println("  Risk: full data disk crashes the OS and logging");

        // CORRECT: separate data volume
        System.out.println("CORRECT: /var/kafka/data on dedicated volume");
        System.out.println("  Safe: OS stays up even if data disk fills");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WRONG: rm /var/kafka/data/transfers-3/*.log
  Risk: corrupts partition, broker may crash
CORRECT: kafka-topics --alter --config retention.ms=86400000
  Safe: Kafka's log cleaner handles deletion properly
WRONG: /var/kafka/data on root filesystem
  Risk: full data disk crashes the OS and logging
CORRECT: /var/kafka/data on dedicated volume
  Safe: OS stays up even if data disk fills
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Tiered storage largely prevents this class of incident for high-retention topics by keeping only recent segments locally. Spread partitions and data evenly because an unbalanced cluster can fill one broker while others are near empty. Separate the Kafka data volume from the operating-system volume so a full data disk does not also crash the OS. Alert on disk usage trend, not just absolute level, to catch gradual growth before it becomes urgent.
